Example ID: 10331115
QUESTION:
Is amoxapine an atypical antipsychotic?

NUMBERED CONTEXT SENTENCES:
[0] All currently available atypical antipsychotics have, at clinically relevant doses: i) high serotonin (5-HT)2 occupancy;
[1] ii) greater 5-HT2 than dopamine (D)2 occupancy;
[2] and iii) a higher incidence of extrapyramidal side effects when their D2 occupancy exceeds 80%.
[3] A review of pharmacologic and behavioral data suggested that amoxapine should also conform to this profile;
[4] therefore, we undertook a positron-emission tomography (PET) study of its 5-HT2 and D2 occupancy.
[5] Seven healthy volunteers received 50-250 mg/day of amoxapine for 5 days and then had [11C]-raclopride and [18F]-setoperone PET scans.
[6] 5-HT2 receptors showed near saturation at doses of 100 mg/day and above.
[7] The D2 receptor occupancies showed a dose-dependent increase, never exceeding 80%;
[8] at all doses 5-HT2 occupancy exceeded D2 occupancy.

